    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The escalating global energy demand, driven by population growth and economic development, necessitates the urgent adoption of sustainable energy solutions. Solar power, particularly photovoltaic (PV) technologies, emerges as a key contender due to its abundance and eco-friendliness. While solar energy has vast potential, a significant portion remains untapped, especially in indoor environments where the Internet of Things (IoT) is rapidly expanding. Existing indoor photovoltaic (IPV) technologies like amorphous silicon, dye-sensitized solar cells, organic photovoltaics, and perovskites each have limitations in efficiency, stability, or scalability. Transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) present a promising alternative due to their high optical absorption, suitable band gaps, self-passivated surfaces, and potential for low-cost production. However, a comprehensive understanding of the theoretical efficiency limits of TMD-based solar cells is crucial to guide material and device optimization efforts. In this work, we aim to establish these limits and assess the viability of TMDs for various applications. First, we establish the efficiency limits of multilayer TMD solar cells (MoS2, MoSe2, WS2, and WSe2) under the standard AM 1.5 G spectrum. Our approach involves an extended detailed balance model that incorporates radiative, Auger, and defect-assisted Shockley-Read-Hall recombination mechanisms, along with measured optical absorption spectra. We demonstrate that even with current material quality, single-junction TMD solar cells with film thicknesses as thin as 50 nm can achieve up to 25% power conversion efficiency. This finding positions TMDs as a strong candidate for high-specific-power photovoltaic applications, particularly in areas like aerospace, transportation, and wearable electronics. Next, we investigate the potential of TMD solar cells for indoor energy harvesting, considering various indoor lighting conditions (fluorescent, LED, halogen, and low-light AM 1.5 G) and illumination levels. Our analysis reveals that TMD solar cells can achieve high power conversion efficiencies under these conditions, comparable to or outperforming incumbent IPV technologies. Specifically, we find that TMDs could reach efficiencies up to 36.5% under fluorescent lighting, 35.6% under LED lighting, 11.2% under halogen lighting, and 27.6% under low-light AM 1.5 G. These results highlight the promise of TMDs as a stable and sustainable material for powering IoT devices and other indoor applications, potentially revolutionizing the future of energy harvesting in indoor environments.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
